nes 15. So the total would be 1,787,775?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places in between. Let me do this addition step by step digit by digit carefully:\n\nLet me align both numbers properly:\n\n      9 2 1 1 2 6\n    + 8 6 6 6 4 9\n    ----------------\n\nStarting from the right (ones):\n\n6 +9 =15 → 5, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 2 +4 =6 +1=7 → 7, no carryover.\n\nHundreds:1 +6=7 → 7.\n\nThousands:1 +6=7 →7.\n\nTen-thousands:2 +6=8 →8.\n\nHundred-thousands:9 +8=17 →7, carryover 1.\n\nThen the million place: carryover 1.\n\nSo putting all the digits from right to left: \n\nOnes:5\n\nTens:7\n\nHundreds:7\n\nThousands:7\n\nTen-thousands:8\n\nHundred-thousands:7\n\nMillions:1\n\nSo the number is 1,787,775.\n\nWait a minute, I think I made a mistake in the thousands place. Let me go through each digit again step by step:\n\nLet me write each digit position:\n\nPositions (from right):\n\n1. Ones: 6 +9 =15 → 5, carry 1.\n\n2. Tens: 2 +4 =6 +1 =7 →7, no carry.\n\n3. Hundreds:1 +6 =7 →7.\n\n4. Thousands:1 (from first number's thousands place) +6 (second number's thousands place) =7 →7.\n\n5. Ten-thousands:2 (first number's ten thousands) +6 (second's ten thousands) =8 →8.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands:9 +8 =17 →7, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions: carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred-thousands:7\n\nTen-thousands:8\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:7\n\nOnes:5\n\nSo combining these: 1,787,775. \n\nWait, but when I add 921,126 and 866,649, let me check with another method to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, adding 921,126 + 800,000 = 1,721,126, then adding the remaining 66,649:\n\n1,721,126 + 66,649 = ?\n\n1,721,126 + 60,000 = 1,781,126\n\n1,781,126 +6,649 = 1,787,775. Yes, that matches. So the answer is 1,787,775. \n\nTherefore, the previous step-by-step was correct. I think initially I might have miscounted the places, but after double-checking, it's correct. So the final answer is 1,787,775.\n</think>Let's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n2. The ones place: 4 + 8 