akes 11. So yes, that's 1 in the hundred-thousands and a carryover of 1 to the millions. So the millions place will have that 1.\n\nPutting it all together: starting from the right, the digits are 5 (ones), 5 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 1 (hundred-thousands), and 1 (millions). So the total should be 1,082,585?\n\nWait, let me verify that again step by step to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me write them aligned:\n\n  671774\n+ 410811\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 4 +1 =5 ✔️\n\nTens:7+1=8, but wait the second number's tens digit is actually 1? Wait, let me check the original numbers again. Wait, 671,774 is 6-7-1-7-7-4, so the digits from right are:\n\nOnes:4, tens:7, hundreds:7, thousands:1, ten-thousands:7, hundred-thousands:6.\n\nWait a second, maybe I messed up the places earlier. Let me break down each number properly.\n\nFirst number: 671,774. Breaking down each digit from right:\n\nPosition: Ones (4), Tens (7), Hundreds (7), Thousands (1), Ten-thousands (7), Hundred-thousands (6).\n\nSecond number: 410,811. Breaking down:\n\nOnes (1), Tens (1), Hundreds (8), Thousands (0), Ten-thousands (1), Hundred-thousands (4).\n\nWait, so when I added the tens place earlier, the first number's tens digit is 7 (from 671,774: the second digit from the right is 7?), let me confirm:\n\n671,774:\n\nDigits: 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nWait, that's a mistake. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So 671,774 is 6 (hundred thousand), 7 (ten thousand), 1 (thousand), 7 (hundred), 7 (ten), 4 (one). So the tens place is indeed 7. The second number is 410,811: 4 (hundred thousand), 1 (ten thousand), 0 (thousand), 8 (hundred), 1 (ten), 1 (one). So the tens digit here is 1. \n\nSo adding tens place: 7 (from first) +1 (from second) =8, but earlier I thought it was 15? Wait, that's where the mistake is! Oh no, I must have miscounted earlier. Wait, let's redo this step by step carefully.\n\nLet me write each digit properly:\n\nFirst number: 671,774:\n\n- Hundred thousands: 6\n\n- Ten thousands: 7\n\n- Thousands: 1\n\n- Hundreds:7\n\n- Tens:7\n\n- Ones:4\n\nSecond number:410,811:\n\n- Hundred thousands:4\n\n- Ten thousands:1\n\n- Thousands:0\n\n- Hundreds:8\n\n- Tens:1\n\n- Ones:1\n\nSo adding digit by digit from right (ones to the left):\n\nOnes place: 4 +1 =5. Correct.\n\nTens place:7 +1 =8. No carryover. Wait, earlier I thought it was 15, which was wrong. That was my mistake. So the tens digit is 8, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place:7 (from first) +8 (from second) =15. So write down 5, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:1 (first) +0 (second) + carryover 1 =2. So 2 in thousands place, no carryover.\n\nTen-thousands place:7 (first) +1 (second) =8. So 8 there.\n\nHundred-thousands place:6 +4 =10. So 10 here. That would mean we have a digit 0 in hundred-thousands and carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:5\n\nTens:8\n\nHundreds:5 (since 15, carryover 1)\n\nThousands:2 (1+0+1 carryover?)\n\nWait let me retrace:\n\nLet me do each step again properly:\n\n1. Ones place: 4 +1 =5. Write 5. No carryover.\n\n2. Tens place:7 +1=8. Write 8. No carryover.\n\n3. Hundreds place:7 +8=15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\n4. Thousands place:1 (from first number) +0 (from second) +1 (carryover) =2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\n5. Ten-thousands place:7 (first) +1 (second) =8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands place:6 +4 =10. Write 0 here, carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\nThen, since there's a carryover 1 to the millions, we add that as the next digit.\n\nSo the total is 1 (millions) 0 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 5 (ones). So the number is 1,082,585.\n\nWait, so my initial mistake was in the tens place where I incorrectly added 7 +8 instead of 7 +1. That's why I got confused earlier. So the correct sum should be 1,082,585.\n\nLet m