unt each step carefully. Let me write each digit's place:\n\nLet's break down each number digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 879,974\nBreaking down:\n- Hundred thousands: 8\n- Ten thousands:7\n- Thousands:9\n- Hundreds:9\n- Tens:7\n- Ones:4\n\nSecond number:265,892\nBreaking down:\n- Hundred thousands:2\n- Ten thousands:6\n- Thousands:5\n- Hundreds:8\n- Tens:9\n- Ones:2\n\nNow adding each column starting from the right (ones):\n\nOnes place: 4 +2 =6. Write down 6.\n\nTens place:7 +9=16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place:9 +8=17, plus the carried over 1 makes 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:9 (from first number) +5 (second number) =14, plus 1 carried over is 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nWait, hold on, the thousands place in the first number is 9? Wait let me confirm:\n\nWait, the first number is 879,974. Let me parse each digit:\n\nBreaking 879,974:\n\nPositions from right (ones to hundred thousands):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):4\n\nPosition 2 (tens):7\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):9\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):8\n\nWait a second, maybe I miscounted earlier. Let me write them again properly.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is arising from how the digits are separated. Let me write each number as individual digits:\n\nFirst number: 8 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSecond number:2 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:\n\nLet me align them properly:\n\nHundred thousands:8 +2\n\nTen thousands:7 +6\n\nThousands:9 +5\n\nHundreds:9 +8\n\nTens:7 +9\n\nOnes:4 +2\n\nWait, but addition is done from the ones to the higher places. Let me start over:\n\nStarting from the ones digit:\n\nOnes:4 +2 =6. No carryover. So first digit is 6.\n\nTens:7 +9 =16. So write 6, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:9 +8 =17, plus 1 carried over =18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:9 +5 =14, plus 1 carried over =15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:7 +6 =13, plus 1 carried over =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:8 +2 =10, plus 1 carried over =11. So write 1 and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, that 1 is added as a new digit to the left.\n\nWait, that would make the total digits 7. Let me see:\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:6\n\nTens:6\n\nHundreds:8\n\nThousands:5\n\nTen thousands:4\n\nHundred thousands:1 (from 10+1 carried over, but actually 11, so 1 in the hundred thousands and carryover 1 to millions place?)\n\nWait, let me track each step again step by step carefully.\n\nLet me write it vertically:\n\n          8 7 9 9 7 4\n        + 2 6 5 8 9 2\n        ---------------\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nOnes:4 +2 =6. Write 6. No carryover.\n\nTens place:7 +9 =16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place:9 +8 =17, plus the 1 carried over is 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:9 (from first number) +5 (second number) =14, plus 1 carried over is 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place:7 +6 =13, plus 1 carried over =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place:8 +2 =10, plus 1 carried over =11. Here, we can't write 11 in a single digit, so we write 1 and carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\nSince there are no more digits, the carried over 1 becomes a new digit at the millions place.\n\nSo putting all together, the sum is:\n\n1 (millions) 1 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWait, let me write it step by step:\n\nAfter adding each column with the carryovers:\n\n- Ones:6\n\n- Tens:6 (with carryover 1)\n\n- Hundreds:8 (carryover 1)\n\n- Thousands:5 (carryover1)\n\n- Ten thousands:4 (carryover1)\n\n- Hundred thousands:1 (carryover1)\n\n- Millions:1\n\nSo the total sum is 1,145,866.\n\nWait, let me confirm with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, adding 879,974 + 265,892.\n\nLet me comp