usands), 6 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 3 (ones). Wait, let me check that again. Wait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me recount the digits step by step.\n\nWait, let me do this more carefully. Let's write each digit's place step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 0 + 3 = 3 → 3\n\nTens place: 0 + 4 = 4 → 4\n\nHundreds place: 8 + 8 = 16 → write 6, carryover 1\n\nThousands place: 3 (from first number) + 2 (from second) = 5, plus 1 carried over is 6 → 6\n\nTen-thousands place: 6 (from first number, since 563800 has 6 in the ten thousands place?) Wait, let me check the original numbers again.\n\nWait, maybe I misaligned the digits. Let me write both numbers with their place values clearly:\n\nThe first number is 563,800. Breaking it down:\n\n- Hundred thousands: 5\n- Ten thousands: 6\n- Thousands: 3\n- Hundreds: 8\n- Tens: 0\n- Ones: 0\n\nThe second number is 852,843:\n\n- Hundred thousands: 8\n- Ten thousands: 5\n- Thousands: 2\n- Hundreds: 8\n- Tens: 4\n- Ones: 3\n\nSo when adding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 0 +3 =3\n\nTens: 0 +4=4\n\nHundreds:8+8=16 (write 6, carryover 1)\n\nThousands place: 3 (from first number) + 2 (from second) =5, plus carryover 1 →6\n\nTen-thousands place:6 (from first) +5 (from second) =11 (write 1, carryover 1)\n\nHundred-thousands place:5 +8=13, plus carryover 1 →14. So here, we have 4 in the hundred-thousands and carryover 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that 1 is added as a new digit in the millions place.\n\nSo putting all together:\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred-thousands:4\n\nTen-thousands:1\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:6\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:3\n\nTherefore the sum is 1,416,643.\n\nWait, let me confirm with another method. Maybe adding the numbers directly:\n\n563,800 + 852,843.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n500,000 + 800,000 = 1,300,000\n\n60,000 + 50,000 = 110,000 → total so far 1,410,000\n\n3,000 + 2,000 = 5,000 → 1,415,000\n\n800 + 800 = 1,600 → 1,416,600\n\n0 +40 =40 → 1,416,640\n\n0 +3 =3 → total 1,4